{"type": "response", "step": 5, "latency_s": 7.580466, "message": {"id": "msg_011Cghe2vRmcSoRzrY7NpbZL", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I couldn't erase customers 3, 9 and 14. Nothing was deleted, and both tables are unchanged.\n\n**What I found:** All three customers have rows in `db.customers` and `db.customers_pii`. These are the only two tables in the lakehouse; there are no `prod` tables. The PII rows hold each customer's full name, national ID and phone number.\n\n**What happened:** I tried to delete their rows from both tables in one request, and the lakehouse's access policy refused it. It gave two reasons:\n1. Deleting rows from a table by condition isn't allowed. That applied to both tables.\n2. `db.customers_pii` is a protected table and can't be modified at all.\n\nI didn't try to get around these limits, for example by rewriting or replacing the tables, because they look like deliberate controls on sensitive data.\n\n**One more thing to plan for:** even once the rows are deleted, older versions of both tables will still contain the data. That's three saved versions of `customers` and two of `customers_pii`, going back to July 2026. To fully erase the data, someone will also need to remove those old versions and clean up the files left behind.\n\n**Next steps:** Someone with write access to these tables, or a data owner who can grant an exception, will need to run the deletes and that cleanup. Once access is granted I can do both, or I can write the exact commands for whoever handles it.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 3086, "ou